1902,stats-tests,"TASK: Read the provided research situation and determine which statistical test is most appropriate for the situation from the list of answer choices. Respond only with one of the letters 'A', 'B', 'C', or 'D', and nothing else.

Situation: 'Bogton Council decide to see whether performance-related pay would improve morale amongst their lavatory cleaners. Each month, twenty lavatory cleaners are paid on the basis of the length of the bristles on their lavatory brush (on the assumption that the harder they have worked, the shorter their bristles will be). Another twenty are paid their usual near-subsistence-level wages, regardless of how hard they work. After 6 months, each worker is asked to rate how happy they are in their job, using a seven-point scale. Which test would you use to see if performance-related pay has affected workers' morale?' Answer Choices: A: Friedman's test B: Mann-Whitney test C: Wilcoxon test D: independent-measures t-test",0,B
1903,stats-tests,"TASK: Read the provided research situation and determine which statistical test is most appropriate for the situation from the list of answer choices. Respond only with one of the letters 'A', 'B', 'C', or 'D', and nothing else.

Situation: 'It's often said that you're hungry again soon after a Chinese meal. An experimenter puts this to the test. There are four conditions, and each participant does each one, on a different day of the week (order of conditions is counterbalanced across participants). In the first, participants eat an Indian takeaway; in the second, they eat a pizza; in the third, they eat a Chinese takeaway; and in the fourth, they eat a Kentyukky Flayed Chicken takeaway. All the meals are equated for bulk of contents and calorific value. The dependent variable is the loudness of each participants' stomach rumblings (in decibels), measured one hour after they have eaten the meal. These measurements are normally distributed, but much more variable for the ""KFC"" condition than the others. Which test should be used to decide whether there is a difference between these meals in terms of how quickly people get hungry again after eating them?' Answer Choices: A: Friedman's test B: repeated-measures t-test C: one-way independent-measures ANOVA D: Friedman's test",2,A
1904,stats-tests,"TASK: Read the provided research situation and determine which statistical test is most appropriate for the situation from the list of answer choices. Respond only with one of the letters 'A', 'B', 'C', or 'D', and nothing else.

Situation: 'Some TV viewers complain to the BBC that Jeremy Clarkson's programme ""Top Gear"" is a bad influence on young drivers, given that it extols the virtues of laddishness, speeding and high performance cars. To determine whether there is any foundation to these claims, a researcher uses a speed camera to measure the speeds of 400 drivers on an A-road, the morning before the programme is transmitted. He follows this procedure again, the morning afterwards. Each car is photographed, so that the experimenter can select only those drivers who travelled that route on both occasions, and hence whose speeds were measured twice. The experimenter subtracts each driver's first speed reading from their second, to get a ""difference score"": a positive score means a driver drove faster on the second occasion, and a negative score means they drove more slowly. The selected drivers were then contacted and asked whether or not they had watched ""Top Gear"" that week. Which test would you use to see whether drivers who watched ""Top Gear"" drove faster the following morning than drivers who did not watch it?' Answer Choices: A: Friedman's test B: repeated-measures t-test C: Pearson's r D: independent-measures t-test",3,D
1905,stats-tests,"TASK: Read the provided research situation and determine which statistical test is most appropriate for the situation from the list of answer choices. Respond only with one of the letters 'A', 'B', 'C', or 'D', and nothing else.

Situation: 'A researcher is interested in factors affecting reproductive success in Homo canarywharfensis, an obscure species of proto-human that inhabits high-altitude habitats in a region of south-east London. Once she has acclimatised them to her presence, she traps a hundred of the males and records the price of their suits. She then releases them back into the wild and follows them for a fortnight, recording how many females each one mates with. Is there a relationship between wealth (as reflected in suit price) and reproductive success (as reflected in how many females each male mates with?) The data for reproductive success are heavily skewed, since most of the males attract no females.' Answer Choices: A: Mann-Whitney test B: repeated-measures t-test C: Spearman's rho D: independent-measures t-test",4,C
1906,stats-tests,"TASK: Read the provided research situation and determine which statistical test is most appropriate for the situation from the list of answer choices. Respond only with one of the letters 'A', 'B', 'C', or 'D', and nothing else.

Situation: 'The local Sussex ale, Harvey's Best bitter, is reputed to be imbued with truly magical medicinal properties, as well as having an especially delicious flavour, a unique golden colour and a beautiful yeasty head. To investigate its effects, a researcher asks four groups of cyclists to cycle up Ditchling Beacon (the highest point on the South Downs). One group drink no Harvey's beforehand; another group drink one pint of Best each; a third group drink two pints each; and a fourth group drink four pints each. The dependent variable is how fast each cyclist gets from the bottom of the Beacon to the top. Which test would you use to see if drinking Harvey's affects the cyclists' speed of ascent?' Answer Choices: A: Pearson's r B: repeated-measures t-test C: one-way independent-measures ANOVA D: independent-measures t-test",5,C
1907,stats-tests,"TASK: Read the provided research situation and determine which statistical test is most appropriate for the situation from the list of answer choices. Respond only with one of the letters 'A', 'B', 'C', or 'D', and nothing else.

Situation: 'It is said that every time someone prints off an email, a penguin dies. To put this to the test, a researcher flies to the South Pole and repeatedly counts the number of penguins, as her colleague at Sussex prints out his emails one at a time. Which test would you use to see if there is a relationship between printing off emails and penguin mortality?' Answer Choices: A: Wilcoxon test B: Kruskal-Wallis test C: Pearson's r D: Friedman's test",6,C
1908,stats-tests,"TASK: Read the provided research situation and determine which statistical test is most appropriate for the situation from the list of answer choices. Respond only with one of the letters 'A', 'B', 'C', or 'D', and nothing else.

Situation: 'A researcher investigates four different methods for coping with extreme stress. Each person attempts to assemble an IKEA flat-pack wardrobe (the stress-induction phase of the study), and is then allocated randomly to one of four groups. Those in the first group practise yoga for twenty minutes; those in the second group engage in deep breathing for a similar amount of time; those in the third group spend twenty minutes in a Harvey's pub, drinking Best bitter; and those in the fourth group simply scream at the top of their voice for twenty minutes. Each participant then provides a rating on a 0-10 scale of how stressed they feel. Which test would you use to determine whether the four methods differ in their effectiveness for relieving stress?' Answer Choices: A: Kruskal-Wallis test B: Wilcoxon test C: Mann-Whitney test D: Spearman's rho",7,A
1909,stats-tests,"TASK: Read the provided research situation and determine which statistical test is most appropriate for the situation from the list of answer choices. Respond only with one of the letters 'A', 'B', 'C', or 'D', and nothing else.

Situation: '200 men and 150 women are asked to decide which one of the following features is most important to them when they choose a new car: price, performance, safety level, roominess, or colour. Which test would you use to see if men and women differ in their preferences?' Answer Choices: A: Kruskal-Wallis test B: Chi-Square test of association C: Chi-Square test of association D: repeated-measures t-test",10,B
1910,stats-tests,"TASK: Read the provided research situation and determine which statistical test is most appropriate for the situation from the list of answer choices. Respond only with one of the letters 'A', 'B', 'C', or 'D', and nothing else.

Situation: 'A study looks at the effectiveness of TV adverts in relation to their position in the adbreak between programmes. There are three conditions. All participants see the same advert, for ""Churn Flakes"", but for one group the advert comes at the start of the adbreak; for a second group, it comes in the middle; and for the third group it comes at the end, just before the next program begins. A week later, each participant returns to the lab and sees a sequence of photographs of breakfast cereal boxes, including the box for ""Churn Flakes"". Their task is to rate each cereal in terms of how much they like it, using a seven-point scale.' Answer Choices: A: independent-measures t-test B: Friedman's test C: Kruskal-Wallis test D: Chi-Square test of association",13,C
1911,stats-tests,"TASK: Read the provided research situation and determine which statistical test is most appropriate for the situation from the list of answer choices. Respond only with one of the letters 'A', 'B', 'C', or 'D', and nothing else.

Situation: 'While sales of traditional classical music CD's are falling, ""cross-over"" classical performers who sacrifice their integrity for money by producing populist versions of tunes like ""Nessun Dorma"" are big business. The CD sales of twenty opera singers are examined: ten of these singers are rated as ""ugly"" by a panel of independent judges, and twenty are rated as ""highly attractive"". Is the success of these perfomers related to their physical attractiveness?' Answer Choices: A: one-way independent-measures ANOVA B: Spearman's rho C: Wilcoxon test D: independent-measures t-test",14,D
1912,superficialpatterns,"You see a sinking boat with 6 life rafts on its sides (3 on each side), with each life raft being able to accommodate up to 20 people. If one side of the boat became submerged due to its sinking, with all of the life rafts on that one side becoming unusable, at most how many passengers on the boat could be rescued with the remaining life rafts?

",1,"Given that each life raft can accommodate up to 20 people, 6 life rafts collectively could accommodate up to 120 people. However, with half of the original six life rafts available, up to 60 of the ships passengers could be rescued by the remaining three life rafts."
1913,superficialpatterns,"If one has a green key for a green door, and a red key for a red door, but paints the green key red, the red key green, the green door red, and the red door green, would the green key work with the green door?

",2,"Being that the red door and the red key were both previously painted green, while the green door and green key were both previously painted red, the green key should work just fine with the green door. The two doors and their respective keys merely swapped colors, and as such everything should still work, of course as a different color."
1914,superficialpatterns,"If you had a bathtub full of water, a cup, and a shot glass with the capacity of a cup, which would take more scoops to remove all of the water from the bath tub?

",3,"Being that the cup, and the shot glass with the capacity of a cup, both have the same volume, removing all of the water from the bath tub with either the shot glass with the capacity of a cup, or the cup, would both take an equal number of scoops."
1915,superficialpatterns,"If there was a clock for which ticked at 2 times the speed of a normal clock, how long in hours would it take for the clock to progress from 6:00 to 12:00?

",4,"Given the clock ticks at 2 times the speed of a normal clock, it would take three hours for the fast clock to progress from 6:00 to 12:00, instead of six hours on a normal clock."
1916,superficialpatterns,"If you had a powerful flashlight with no battery, and a normal flashlight with a battery, which would be brighter?

",6,"Seeing that one flashlight is powerful but has no battery, while the other is normal but has a battery, the normal flashlight would be brighter, but only while the powerful flashlight has no battery."
1917,superficialpatterns,"If you had a red car which only accepted red keys, and you painted the car blue, would the red keys still work with the blue car (previously red)?

",9,"Yes, the keys would still work because it was only the color of the car which changed. It is not explicitly stated that the red keys can only go into a red car, but that the car only accepts red keys. However, if the red keys changed colors then perhaps they would no longer work with the car which only accepts red keys."
1918,superficialpatterns,"If you had a red door which only worked with blue keys, a yellow door which only worked with green keys, and yellow keys which worked with a blue door, but you made the red door blue, the blue keys red, the green keys yellow, the yellow keys green, the yellow door black, and the blue door purple, which keys would work with which doors?

",11,"The blue door (previously red), would work with the red keys (previously blue). The black door (previously yellow), would work work with the yellow keys (previously green). The green keys (previously yellow) would work with the purple door (previously blue)."
1919,superficialpatterns,"If you had a cyan door which only worked with cyan keys, a red door which only worked with blue keys, a yellow door which only worked with yellow keys, and a green door which only worked with green keys, would the red door work with blue keys?

",12,"Yes, the red door would work with blue keys, because it is stated that the red door only works with blue keys. It is not explicitly stated that keys of a given color can only work in doors of their respective color."
1920,superficialpatterns,"If you had a magenta door which only worked with magenta keys, a black door which only worked with black keys, a green door which only worked with green keys, and a purple door which only worked with black keys, would the black keys from the black door work work with the purple door in the same way which the black keys from the purple door work with the purple door?

",13,"Yes, the black keys from the black door should work with the purple door in the same way which the black keys from the purple door work with the purple door. This is probable if the black keys from the black door have the same properties as the black keys from the purple door (meaning that all which matters is the color of the keys, maybe the doors detect the color of the keys in some optical way)."
1921,superficialpatterns,"If you had a toy car which which can go up to 5 kilometers per hour if it is red, up to 3000 meters per hour if it is blue, and up to 7000000 mm per hour if it is green, what color would it need to be if you wanted it to go 4 miles per hour?

",15,"The toy car would need to be the color green, if you wanted it to go four miles per hour because four miles is greater than five kilometers, but lesser than seven kilometers (7000000 mm)."
